    "body": "## Summary\n\n`ao browser screenshot <path> --json` accepts the positional path but silently ignores it. It writes no PNG and prints the entire image as base64 JSON, which can flood and truncate agent tool output.\n\n**Reported by:** external AO user; report relayed by the project operator (public handle/source URL not supplied)  \n**Observed:** 2026-08-31  \n**Analyzed against:** `main` at `ab17197b0d1bdc8d4d9849ef46675e067fc77119`  \n**Confidence:** High; current CLI implementation has this exact branch.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```sh\nao browser screenshot /tmp/ao-browser-test.png --json\ntest -f /tmp/ao-browser-test.png\n```\n\nObserved:\n\n- command returns a very large `result.data` base64 field;\n- the requested file does not exist.\n\nThe user report measured roughly 46,000 tokens before truncation.\n\n## Root cause and contract conflict\n\nIn `backend/internal/cli/browser.go`, screenshot handling calls the browser action first and then immediately serializes the full response when `jsonOutput` is true. Positional-path handling exists only in the non-JSON branch.\n\nThe bundled browser guide currently documents base64 for `--json`, but the Cobra contract says `screenshot [path]` and describes the command as capturing to a PNG file. Accepting a path and silently discarding it is unsafe and surprising even if the behavior was documented elsewhere.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- When a path is supplied, write the PNG in both text and JSON modes.\n- JSON output for a file write returns compact metadata: resolved path, byte size, width, and height.\n- Inline image data requires an explicit opt-in such as `--base64`; it is never the accidental result of combining a path with `--json`.\n- Preserve overwrite protections and path-validation behavior.\n- Add CLI tests for absolute/relative paths, `--json`, file bytes, compact output, overwrite refusal, and explicit inline mode.\n- Update the bundled browser guide and help text so they agree.\n",
